WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.290
The Star Wars series includes some of the
biggest blockbusters of all time, due in no

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:08.170
small part to the rabid and meticulous fandom
that’s followed it through the years.

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:11.800
Even after a thousand viewings, there are
still some things you may have missed in that

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:13.280
galaxy far, far away.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:16.910
Here are a few hidden details in Star Wars
movies.

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:18.360
Lucas Family Cameos

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:22.470
George Lucas resisted the temptation to go
Hitchcock during the original Star Wars movies

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:26.750
and make cameo appearances, but he did allow
himself a few indulgences during the prequels.

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:29.539
His son and daughter appear in the prequel
trilogy.

00:00:29.539 --> 00:00:33.290
That's Jett Lucas you see in the library in
Attack of the Clones, and again kicking clone

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:37.250
trooper butt when the Jedi younglings are
attacked during Revenge of the Sith.

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:41.280
Lucas himself appears alongside his daughter
Katie in the latter film, playing the blue-skinned

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:46.579
Baron Notluwiski Papanoida, who became a regular
in the animated Clone Wars series.

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:48.230
And speaking of cameos...

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:49.490
Boba Fett Unmasked

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:54.219
Officially, Star Wars fans didn't get their
first look at a helmet-less Boba Fett until

00:00:54.219 --> 00:00:56.590
the second prequel, Attack of the Clones.

00:00:56.590 --> 00:01:00.559
But the guy who played Fett in the original
films, Jeremy Bulloch, actually showed his

00:01:00.559 --> 00:01:02.370
face in The Empire Strikes Back.

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:06.289
When another actor failed to show up for filming
during the shoot, Bulloch stepped in to play

00:01:06.289 --> 00:01:10.439
an Imperial officer who drags Leia away while
she's trying to warn Luke about Vader's trap

00:01:10.439 --> 00:01:11.439
in Bespin.

00:01:11.439 --> 00:01:15.490
Years later, Bulloch returned for another
non-Fett cameo, playing the pilot who delivers

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:20.069
Obi-Wan Kenobi, Bail Organa, and Yoda to Coruscant
in Revenge of the Sith.

00:01:20.069 --> 00:01:22.200
The Fett legacy doesn’t end there, however.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.479
Klutzy Stormtrooper DNA

00:01:24.479 --> 00:01:28.969
Hardcore Star Wars fans know about the Stormtrooper
head-bump goof-up that made it into the final

00:01:28.969 --> 00:01:29.969
cut of A New Hope.

00:01:29.969 --> 00:01:34.109
Rather than correct the mistake, George Lucas
paid tribute to it when he had the character

00:01:34.109 --> 00:01:38.439
of Jango Fett—whose DNA was used to create
the first Clone Trooper army—whack his head

00:01:38.439 --> 00:01:40.689
on the door as he climbs into his ship.

00:01:40.689 --> 00:01:45.039
Clone Troopers were eventually phased out
for conscripted Stormtroopers, but some Clone

00:01:45.039 --> 00:01:46.450
Troopers remained in service.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:48.399
Guess we know who this guy’s dad was.

00:01:48.399 --> 00:01:50.569
Cellblock 1138

00:01:50.569 --> 00:01:55.090
Years before embarking on the Star Wars saga,
George Lucas made his big screen directorial

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:59.749
debut with the 1971 sci-fi feature THX 1138.

00:01:59.749 --> 00:02:03.789
It wasn't a major commercial success, so many
viewers initially missed the significance

00:02:03.789 --> 00:02:08.509
when Luke Skywalker makes a reference the
Death Star’s "cellblock 1138" during A New

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:09.509
Hope.

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:11.340
“Where are you taking this…

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:12.340
Thing.”

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:16.920
“Prisoner transfer from cell block 1138.”

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:18.019
Corellian Freighters

00:02:18.019 --> 00:02:21.879
It’s easy to believe that the Millennium
Falcon is a one-of-a-kind spaceship.

00:02:21.879 --> 00:02:25.530
After all, while we saw plenty of X-wings
and TIE fighters zipping through space, we

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:27.870
never got a look at anything like another
Falcon.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:32.659
But the truth is that Han Solo's beloved freighter
was just one of many in the Corellian YT-1300

00:02:32.659 --> 00:02:33.659
line.

00:02:33.659 --> 00:02:37.060
George Lucas slipped in several of them in
the background during the prequels—including

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:41.700
a brief shot of the Falcon herself, spotted
docking at Coruscant long before Solo claimed

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:42.700
ownership.

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:47.040
That’s not the only hidden detail featuring
the fastest hunk of junk in the galaxy...

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:49.239
Millennium Falcon's New Dish

00:02:49.239 --> 00:02:52.720
We don't know much about what happened to
the Millennium Falcon after it lost its radar

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:54.150
dish in Return of the Jedi…

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:55.870
“That was too close.”

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:59.459
...but it's obvious that someone did a little
repair work along the way.

00:02:59.459 --> 00:03:03.249
The ship was given a rectangular replacement
that you can clearly see during The Force

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:04.249
Awakens.

00:03:04.249 --> 00:03:06.659
Maybe it was installed by the same person
who worked on C-3PO?

00:03:06.659 --> 00:03:11.420
“You probably don’t recognise me because
of of the red arm.”

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:12.450
Vader's Helmet

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:16.689
When Palpatine invites Anakin into his private
booth during a performance of the "bubble

00:03:16.689 --> 00:03:20.680
opera" during Revenge of the Sith, the audience
already knows the young Jedi will be corrupted

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:21.959
into becoming Darth Vader.

00:03:21.959 --> 00:03:26.459
But to add that extra little bit of nearly
subliminal foreshadowing, George Lucas inserted

00:03:26.459 --> 00:03:29.980
an almost imperceptible glimpse of Vader's
helmet into the show.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:33.359
Some viewers swear they can even hear what
sounds like a couple seconds of Vader breathing

00:03:33.359 --> 00:03:35.520
way down in the mix.

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:36.520
Potato asteroids

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:41.170
For years, rumors have persisted that the
Empire Strikes Back effects crew, annoyed

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:46.390
by George Lucas' almost impossible demands,
decided to strike back by using a potato and

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:50.190
a sneaker as "asteroids" during a Millennium
Falcon chase sequence.

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:54.680
Years later, it was revealed that the potato
story was actually true—an in-joke between

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:58.739
workers who'd developed countless asteroid
designs only to be told that the end result

00:03:58.739 --> 00:04:01.099
looked like a certain starchy food.

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:05.519
“Potatoes, boil-em, mash-em, stick-em in
a stew.”

00:04:05.519 --> 00:04:06.739
Skywalker Sound

00:04:06.739 --> 00:04:11.239
There's a lot going on in Coruscant, making
it easy for Lucas to sneak in the logo for

00:04:11.239 --> 00:04:15.249
his Skywalker Sound company during a brief
shot of the city in Attack of the Clones.

00:04:15.249 --> 00:04:19.470
Take a look at the blue, circular logo at
the top-right section of the screen.

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:20.470
Busted!

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:22.250
Ralph McQuarrie's Cameo

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:26.830
George Lucas dreamed up the Star Wars galaxy,
but the iconic designs that amazed generations

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:29.350
of moviegoers came from designer Ralph McQuarrie.

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:33.470
His concept art introduced a number of key
elements to the series, and were instrumental

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:36.949
in Lucas landing a distribution deal with
20th Century Fox.

00:04:36.949 --> 00:04:41.139
McQuarrie's presence is felt throughout the
saga, but he actually appears in person during

00:04:41.139 --> 00:04:42.510
a scene in Empire Strikes Back.

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:47.380
He can be briefly spotted as a Rebel officer
walking through the base on Hoth.

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:48.380
R2-KT

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:54.840
A pink version of R2-D2, R2-KT was originally
a fan community project designed to help lift

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:58.759
the spirits of Katie Johnson, a young Star
Wars enthusiast who'd been diagnosed with

00:04:58.759 --> 00:05:00.590
an inoperable brain tumor.

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:04.380
Dreamed up by her dad as a way of giving Katie
a bit of comfort during her final days, the

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:08.199
droid received her official live action feature
film debut during The Force Awakens, making

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:12.020
brief appearances during scenes set on the
Resistance base.

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:13.480
I guess there's hope for humanity after all

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:14.620
Thanks for watching!

00:05:14.629 --> 00:05:17.979
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:05:17.979 --> 00:05:21.639
And leave us a comment to let us know what
hidden details in Star Wars you think are

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:22.140
the coolest…

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:24.780
*cough* I like coughed as I finished saying that sentence.

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:29.840
I'm gonna keep that in because I'm a rebel. Get it? Star Wars joke? I'm gonna go now.

